---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PixelArt from "../components/PixelArt.astro";
import { REPO } from "../data/site";

const sample = `{
  "coverage_status": "unavailable",
  "confidence": "low",
  "evidence_count": 0,
  "safety_score": null,
  "risk_level": "unknown"
}`;
---

<Base title="Nayki">
  <section class="sec black hero--video">
    <video
      class="hero__video"
      data-hero-video
      autoplay
      muted
      loop
      playsinline
      preload="metadata"
      poster="/timeline-poster.jpg"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <source src="/timeline.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
    </video>
    <div class="hero__scrim"></div>
    <div class="wrap hero__copy" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow hero__top">Open source · In development · Android first</p>
      <div class="hero__row">
        <h1>Know what the <span class="hl">route</span> knows.</h1>
        <div>
          <p class="lede">
            Nayki is a navigation app that shows the evidence behind each route: lighting, nearby help, reported
            incidents and time of day. It never calls a place safe. When it has no data, it says so.
          </p>
          <div class="btns">
            <a class="btn" href="/contribute">Help build it</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={REPO} rel="noopener">View the source</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">What it is</p>
        <h2>A map that shows its homework.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most maps give you one line and a time. Nayki is built to give you the line, the alternatives, and what is
          known about each. You choose.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figwrap" data-reveal>
        <PixelArt
          scene="nightwalk"
          tone="dark"
          label="Pixel street map. A dashed fast route from A to B crosses a rose patch marked RISK. A longer rose route follows lit streets past a help point, with a walker marked YOU."
          fig="Fig. 1"
          caption="A walk from A to B, shown at night. The fast route (dashed) cuts through a dark junction that missing street lights and reports mark as higher risk. The lit route is longer, keeps to lit streets and passes a help point. Nayki lays both out so you choose."
          legend={[
            { kind: "rose", text: "Lit route, with you walking it" },
            { kind: "dash", text: "Faster route" },
            { kind: "rose", text: "Higher-risk patch (RISK)" },
            { kind: "ink", text: "Help point (+) and street lights" },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
      <div class="cards" data-reveal>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3>Compare routes</h3>
          <p>Side-by-side routes with the reasons behind each risk level, not a single score you have to trust.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3>See where help is</h3>
          <p>A help layer for places that can assist: open venues, transport points and services near the route.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3>Report what you saw</h3>
          <p>Incident reports are kept and counted as unverified user reports until something stronger backs them.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="num">04</span>
          <h3>SOS in one tap</h3>
          <p>An SOS flow designed around saved contacts and push notifications. It supports, and never replaces, calling emergency services.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec forest">
    <div class="wrap split split--flip">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The rule</p>
        <h2>It never says <span class="hl">safe</span>.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Nayki reports lower, moderate or higher risk, or unknown, with a confidence value and a count of the evidence
          behind it. Where nothing is known, the answer is "unavailable", not an invented comfort.
        </p>
        <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>
        <p class="note" style="margin-top:28px">
          This is the response the backend returns when it has no safety evidence for a place. It is the API's own rule,
          written in the README.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <PixelArt
          scene="heat"
          tone="dark"
          label="A grid of areas shaded as low, mid or high risk, with hollow cells marked NONE where there is no evidence."
          fig="Fig. 2"
          caption="A neighbourhood split into areas. Each area gets a level from its own evidence. Hollow cells have too little evidence to say anything, so they stay unknown instead of being called safe."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec blush">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it stands</p>
        <h2>Honest status.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Some of this runs, some of it is a prototype, and some of it is still to be connected.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tbl-wrap" data-reveal>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Part</th><th>Status</th><th>What that means</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Android app</td>
              <td><span class="pill pill--rose">Prototype</span></td>
              <td>Jetpack Compose screens for map, search, route comparison, heatmap, help layer, incidents, SOS and profile. They run on sample data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Backend API</td>
              <td><span class="pill pill--solid">In the repo</span></td>
              <td>FastAPI with Postgres/PostGIS and Redis. Endpoints for auth, routes, places, incidents, safety, SOS and privacy, with tests.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>App to API link</td>
              <td><span class="pill">Not yet</span></td>
              <td>The app does not call the backend yet. Connecting them is the first big job.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Real safety data</td>
              <td><span class="pill">Not yet</span></td>
              <td>Data sources are wired as integrations in code. No city has coverage today.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec forest">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Build it with us</p>
        <h2>Safety tools are better built by many hands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          We need Android and backend developers, people who know open data, designers, accessibility reviewers, and
          teams who work on safety in their city. If that is you, come and build this.
        </p>
        <div class="btns">
          <a class="btn" href="/contribute">See how to join</a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/motive">Why we are building it</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
